#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

char* modifyFileNameExtension(const char* originalFilename) {
    int dotIndex = -1;
    int extensionLength = 0;
    for (int i = 0; originalFilename[i] != '\0'; ++i) {
        if (originalFilename[i] == '.') {
            dotIndex = i;
            break;
        }
        if (isdigit(originalFilename[i])) ++extensionLength;
    }

    char* newFilename = malloc((strlen(originalFilename) + extensionLength + 4)); // 4 for "v." and the null terminator
    sprintf(newFilename, "%s%s.", originalFilename, dotIndex == -1 ? ".0" : originalFilename + dotIndex);

    return newFilename;
}
```
This implementation assumes that there is a single '.' in the filename to determine the extension and the extension starts with a digit. If this isn't the case, modifications will be needed to accommodate different filenames. The Java method uses a concurrent hashmap and an executor service to handle potential concurrent access to the state object during distributed system operation. The C function modifies the filename extension based on characters in the original filename using sprintf and strcat functions.']

