```java
import org.redisson.Redisson;
import org.redisson.api.RMap;
import org.redisson.api.RedissonClient;
import org.springframework.cloud.vault.VaultException;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import akka.actor.ActorSystem;
import akka.cluster.ClusterSingletonProxy;
import scala.concurrent.duration.FiniteDuration;

@Configuration
public class RoleManager {
    private static RedissonClient redissonClient;
    private static RMap<String, String> roleCache;
    private static ClusterSingletonProxy roleSyncActorRef;

    public static void initialize(RedissonClient redisson) {
        RoleManager.redissonClient = redisson;
        RoleManager.roleCache = redisson.getMap("roles");
        ActorSystem system = ActorSystem.create("RoleSync");
        roleSyncActorRef = ClusterSingletonProxy.get(system, "RoleSync");
    }

    public void updateRole(String serviceId, String role) {
        try {
            String currentRole = getRole(serviceId);
            if (!currentRole.equals(role)) {
                roleCache.put(serviceId, role);
                roleSyncActorRef.tell(new UpdateRoleMessage(serviceId, role), ActorRef.noSender());
            }
        } catch (VaultException e) {
            // Handle Vault exceptions gracefully
        }
    }

    public String getRole(String serviceId) {
        return roleCache.get(serviceId);
    }

    // Define an UpdateRoleMessage class to communicate the role update request to the RoleSync actor
}
```

In this implementation:
- Redis is used as a caching layer, reducing database load and ensuring efficient propagation of role changes across services.
- Spring Cloud Vault is utilized for external service integration.
- Akka Cluster's Singleton Proxy is employed to maintain a single instance of the RoleSync actor, handling the synchronization across multiple microservices.

Regarding cache invalidation strategies:
1. Absolute Expiration Time: Set an expiry time on cached data. This strategy simplifies management but may cause stale data during short network disruptions or service outages.
2. Sliding Expiration Time: Invalidate a portion of the cache every few minutes. This strategy reduces the risk of stale data but may result in increased cache misses and higher database load when updating role information.
3. Eviction Policy: Remove least recently used (LRU) or least frequently used (LFU) items from the cache. This strategy provides a balance between reducing staleness and minimizing cache misses, but requires additional logic to manage the cache effectively.
4. Key-Based Invalidation: Invalidating specific cache entries when certain events occur (e.g., role updates). This strategy allows for granular control over data consistency, but may cause increased database load during peak traffic periods.']

